# Nothing evaluates before it is asked

**DONE.** `huggorm.Warmer` is built and gated. The section at the end
says what it cost, and records the debounce this file argued for -
which a measurement refuted and a gate then un-refuted.

The last third of `tasks/016`, and the last piece of the
destination `CLAUDE.md` names: "registered expressions evaluated
eagerly in the background so a user-triggered eval is already in
progress or already done."

It waited for `tasks/083`, and correctly: re-evaluating eagerly is
only useful once something knows the old answer is stale. That is
built now - `Watcher` decides what to forget and `Notifier` notices
without being asked - so this is what is missing.

## What exists to build on

    Watcher.eval_file(root)    evaluates and records a snapshot
    Watcher.changed(path)      forgets the roots that could depend on it
    Notifier.next_change()     one event, acted on, roots ANSWERED

The last line is the hinge. `changed()` already returns the roots it
forgot, and `next_change()` passes that up. So the trigger is not
missing - what is missing is anything that does something with it.
Today a caller gets a list of stale roots and has to re-evaluate them
itself.

## What it is

A registry of roots to keep warm, and a task that re-evaluates one
when it goes stale.

    register(root)     keep this one warm
    unregister(root)
    ...and a loop over next_change() that re-evaluates what it named

That is small, and the smallness is the point: 083 built the change
source so that this would be a consumer rather than a rewrite.

## What it has to get right, and none of it is obvious

**A re-evaluation is not free and a change is not one event.** Saving
a file raises several inotify events, and an editor writing by rename
raises more. Re-evaluating on each of them evaluates the same root
three or four times. Some debounce is needed, and a debounce is a
TIMER - which is the first thing in this whole area that cannot be
gated without one. That is the design question, not the loop.

**A failed eager evaluation is not a failure.** A file saved
mid-edit does not parse. The eager pass must not raise into whatever
is running, must not retry forever, and must not swallow the error so
completely that a caller asking later gets a fresh copy of the same
failure with no history.

**It shares the state's thread with the user.** An `EvalState` is
affine, so an eager evaluation and a user-triggered one QUEUE behind
each other. Starting one eagerly can therefore make a user wait,
which is the opposite of the point. Whether the eager pass yields, or
runs on its own state, or is simply cancelled when a real call
arrives, is a decision this task has to make and record.

**Cancelling an evaluation in flight.** libexpr offers no such thing.
So "cancelled" can only mean "we stopped waiting for it", and the
evaluation keeps running on that thread. Worth measuring before
designing around it.

## Where it lives

Beside `Notifier`, under both surfaces, for the reason Carl gave for
`Watcher`: it is written against `EvalStateLike`, so one object serves
the in-process and the remote case. It needs no C++.

## Where it does not go

`tasks/085` is the other open piece of the destination, and it is a
different problem: a process-wide log sink needs C++ and is blocked on
approval. This one is blocked on nothing.

## 2026-09-03: built

**DONE.** `huggorm.Warmer`, in `packages/huggorm/huggorm/warm.py`.
Seven gates in `tests/test_warm.py`, and the suite went from 327 to
336 - two of the nine are `tasks/087`'s, found on the way.

    Warmer(watcher)
      .register(root) / .unregister(root)
      .stale()          registered roots the watcher has dropped
      .refresh()        evaluate them; answers the ones that worked
      .follow(source)   refresh whenever the source says so
      .failures         what the last eager attempt raised, per root

### `stale()` is derived, and that is the design

This file expected a registry plus a staleness flag. The flag is not
needed: `Watcher.changed()` DELETES a forgotten root's snapshot, so
"registered and has no snapshot" already means "its answer is gone".

So the module records nothing about staleness and there is nothing for
it and the watcher to disagree about. It is the same move `tasks/083`
made when it read `Watcher.roots` rather than tracking its own.

### What this file got wrong about the debounce, twice

The section above said the design question was the debounce: one save
is several inotify events, so a refresh per event evaluates one root
several times, and a timer is needed to coalesce them. That was
argued, so it was measured.

**The measurement said the debounce was pointless.** A drain loop
reading changes across a save saw exactly ONE, in-place or by rename.
The reasoning fitted: the first event forgets the root, which drops
its snapshot, which empties `Watcher.watching()`, so every later event
of the burst matches nothing. The window was removed and the module
became timer-free.

**The measurement was wrong, and the gate caught it.** The probe never
REFRESHED between events. `follow()` does, and a refresh re-evaluates
the root and re-watches its files - which is precisely what lets the
straggler through. `test_one_save_is_one_evaluation` failed with two
evaluations for one save, and the window went back in.

Worth keeping because the wrong step was a real measurement rather
than a guess. It measured the right thing in the wrong harness, and
only a gate driving the actual loop could tell.

### A failure is recorded, not raised

An eager pass has no caller to raise to, and a file saved mid-edit
that does not parse is the normal case rather than an error. So
`refresh()` records the exception against the root and leaves the root
STALE.

Stale is the half that matters. A failure marking the root warm would
leave it never re-evaluated, and the caller asking later would get an
answer built from a file that no longer says that. Gated.

Leaving it stale also means the next change tries again, which is one
attempt per change rather than a retry loop - so `refresh()` is
edge-driven, and calling it in a loop is not what it is for.

### What building it found

**`tasks/087`.** A failed evaluation leaves its files CACHED, so a
file fixed after a bad save keeps raising the old error - and a failed
evaluation records no snapshot, so no later change can implicate that
root. The wedge was permanent and silent. It was found here because an
eager pass evaluates half-saved files as a matter of course, and it is
a `Watcher` defect rather than this module's. Fixed in the same
branch.

**A gate that waited on the wrong thing.**
`test_a_saved_file_is_re_evaluated_with_nobody_asking` first waited
for `stale()` to empty, which is vacuous: a warm root is already
unstale, so the wait returned before the save was ever noticed and the
test asserted on the old answer. It waits for the EVALUATION now.

### What it does not do, and none of it is fixable here

**It does not make a user's call faster while it runs.** An
`EvalState` is affine, so an eager evaluation holds the watcher's
lock. `refresh()` takes that lock once per ROOT rather than once for
the batch, so a user waits one evaluation rather than the whole
refresh - but never zero. A second `EvalState` for the eager pass is
the only thing that would, and that is a different design.

**It does not cancel an evaluation in flight.** libexpr offers no way
to, so "cancelled" could only mean "stopped waiting", and the
evaluation would run on regardless. Nothing here pretends otherwise.

### After this

The destination in `CLAUDE.md` is reached in its parts: a handle that
outlives its creator, a warm state, a watcher that knows when the
answer is stale, and now something that refreshes it before anyone
asks. What is not built is the sentence as one thing - a second client
claiming a live `EvalState` and finding the work already done. That
wants an end-to-end gate over the rpc rather than new machinery, and
it has no task yet.
